WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:06.300
Syntax and runtime errors also occur when
trying to retrieve data from a database.

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:12.900
Here is a query that will attempt to retrieve
information from a database.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:19.170
When I execute it, we get a syntax error near
the FROM keyword.

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:24.940
Upon further inspection, we see something
common to queries, a misplacement of commas.

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:31.060
The usual reason for this is that a column
was located after this Status column.

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:35.690
It was removed, but the comma was never replaced.

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:41.120
One solution to the problem is always to ensure
there is no comma after the last column listed

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:43.120
in a query.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:49.980
Here is a tip for using commas that will make
the previous issue never happen.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:56.570
It's actually a tip I got from a coworker
long ago: Put the commas at the beginning

00:00:56.570 --> 00:01:08.950
of each line after the first line.

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:17.159
I will do that and remove the commas from
the ends of the lines, execute the statement,

00:01:17.159 --> 00:01:23.729
and the query now works.

00:01:23.729 --> 00:01:26.930
Let us now see an example of a runtime error.

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:32.250
With management applications such as SQL Server
Management Studio, one could be working with

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:34.340
multiple databases.

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:39.710
Sometimes, someone could be trying to run
a well-written query but against the wrong

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:40.710
database.

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:47.390
I am purposely going to switch databases and
then try to execute this query.

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:51.369
Of course, it will not work because the query
is running against the wrong database.

00:01:51.369 --> 00:01:58.902
What is worse is to have two copies of a database,
such as a production copy and a test copy,

00:01:58.902 --> 00:02:03.390
and run an action query, such as an UPDATE,
INSERT, or DELETE query against the wrong

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:05.440
database.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:09.830
One way to solve this problem is to utilize
a USE statement at the beginning of a query.

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:19.660
I will add, to the top of this query, the
statement USE AdventureWorks2019.

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:27.220
Then, when I execute this, everything runs.

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:30.470
The Available Databases field updates as well.

